From: Surface Overdrive <noreply@surface-overdrive>
Subject: [PATCH] ipu3: front camera first; sizes for video calls, only ones that show the whole picture

The cameras are registered from the last CSI-2 receiver to the first, so that the front camera of the Surface Go comes first:
the first camera is the one applications offer first (a browser's camera prompt asked for the rear one).

Each camera offers the picture size chosen in the Cameras page first (Kamoso takes it), the other picture sizes, and 1280x720
for video calls (a browser asking for nothing or 640x480 used to get 2560x1920, 4.9 MP to scale down itself). Only sizes
that show the whole picture are offered: the ImgU's main output cannot scale, so a size the BDS cannot reach from the
sensor came out cropped (measured from pictures, zoom against the largest size: front 1152x864 to 2048x1536 1.06-1.18x,
640x480 1.78x; front 1280x960, 1280x720 and 2560x1920 the whole sensor). The front camera now offers 2560x1920, 1280x960
and 1280x720; the rear keeps 1536x1152 to 3200x2400 and adds 1280x720.

---
diff --git a/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu3/ipu3.cpp b/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu3/ipu3.cpp
index d023ea2..8300a72 100644
--- a/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu3/ipu3.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu3/ipu3.cpp
@@ -552,40 +552,51 @@ PipelineHandlerIPU3::generateConfiguration(Camera *camera, Span<const StreamRole
 			bufferCount = IPU3CameraConfiguration::kBufferCount;
 			streamFormats[pixelFormat] = { { kMinOfferedSize, maxSize } };
 
-			if (data->cio2_.sensor()->model() == "ov8865") {
-				/*
-				 * The rear camera: the 1632x1224 binned sensor mode streams at 30 fps and the ImgU needs
-				 * kOutputMargin* of margin around the picture, so 1536x1152 (aligned to the ImgU) is the
-				 * largest 4:3 size at 30 fps. The PipeWire plugin only lists standard sizes for a range,
-				 * and none of them is both 4:3 and aligned above 1152x864, so list the sizes explicitly.
-				 * Bigger sizes need the full 3264x2448 mode (about 15 fps).
-				 */
-				std::vector<SizeRange> rearSizes;
-				const char *minWidth = getenv("LIBCAMERA_IPU3_REAR_MIN_WIDTH");
-				unsigned int minW = minWidth ? strtoul(minWidth, nullptr, 10) : 0;
+			/*
+			 * The sizes offered: first the picture size chosen in the Cameras page ("min_width" of the live profile,
+			 * LIBCAMERA_IPU3_REAR_MIN_WIDTH for the rear), which applications such as Kamoso simply take, then 1280x720 for
+			 * video calls, which a browser picks by its constraints. Only sizes that show the whole picture are offered:
+			 * the ImgU's main output cannot scale (its GDC size is the output size), so a size the BDS cannot reach from
+			 * the sensor comes out cropped. Measured from pictures (zoom against the largest size): front 2560x1920,
+			 * 1280x960 and 1280x720 show the whole sensor, 1152x864 to 2048x1536 were zoomed 1.06-1.18x and 640x480
+			 * 1.78x; rear 1536x1152 and 2048x1536 1.04x, 1280x720, 1280x960 and 2560x1920 1.10x against 3200x2400.
+			 * A browser that asks for 640x480 gets 1280x960 and scales it itself. The rear's sizes up to 1536x1152
+			 * come from its 1632x1224 mode at 30 fps, bigger ones need the full 3264x2448 mode (15 fps). The PipeWire
+			 * plugin lists explicit sizes only (a range gives standard sizes, none 4:3 and aligned above 1152x864).
+			 */
+			{
+				const bool rear = data->cio2_.sensor()->model() == "ov8865";
 				LiveProfile profile(data->cio2_.sensor()->model());
+				unsigned int minW = 0;
+				const char *minWidth = rear ? getenv("LIBCAMERA_IPU3_REAR_MIN_WIDTH") : nullptr;
+				if (minWidth)
+					minW = strtoul(minWidth, nullptr, 10);
 				if (profile.has("min_width"))
 					minW = std::clamp(profile.number("min_width", 0), 0.0, 10000.0);
-				for (const Size &s : { Size{ 1536, 1152 }, Size{ 2048, 1536 }, Size{ 2560, 1920 }, Size{ 3200, 2400 } })
-					if (s.width >= minW)
-						rearSizes.emplace_back(s);
-				if (rearSizes.empty())
-					rearSizes.emplace_back(Size{ 1536, 1152 });
-				/* The first size is the one applications such as Kamoso take: LIBCAMERA_IPU3_REAR_MIN_WIDTH picks it. */
-				size = rearSizes.front().max;
-				streamFormats[pixelFormat] = rearSizes;
-			} else {
-				/* The front camera: same idea, and "min_width" of its live profile picks the first size offered. */
-				std::vector<SizeRange> frontSizes;
-				LiveProfile profile(data->cio2_.sensor()->model());
-				const unsigned int minW = std::clamp(profile.number("min_width", 0), 0.0, 10000.0);
-				for (const Size &s : { Size{ 1152, 864 }, Size{ 1536, 1152 }, Size{ 2048, 1536 }, Size{ 2560, 1920 } })
-					if (s.width >= minW && s.width <= maxSize.width)
-						frontSizes.emplace_back(s);
-				if (!frontSizes.empty()) {
-					size = frontSizes.front().max;
-					streamFormats[pixelFormat] = frontSizes;
-				}
+
+				const std::vector<Size> pictures = rear
+					? std::vector<Size>{ { 1536, 1152 }, { 2048, 1536 }, { 2560, 1920 }, { 3200, 2400 } }
+					: std::vector<Size>{ { 1280, 960 }, { 2560, 1920 } };
+				const std::vector<Size> calls{ { 1280, 720 } };
+
+				/* the first picture size at least min_width wide (an older profile's 1152, 1536 or 2048 picks the next one) */
+				Size first = pictures.back();
+				for (const Size &s : pictures)
+					if (s.width >= minW) {
+						first = s;
+						break;
+					}
+
+				std::vector<SizeRange> offered{ SizeRange(first) };
+				for (const Size &s : pictures)
+					if (s != first && s.width <= maxSize.width)
+						offered.emplace_back(s);
+				for (const Size &s : calls)
+					if (s.width <= maxSize.width)
+						offered.emplace_back(s);
+
+				size = first;
+				streamFormats[pixelFormat] = offered;
 			}
 
 			break;
@@ -1232,7 +1243,12 @@ int PipelineHandlerIPU3::registerCameras()
 	 * in a compatible format.
 	 */
 	unsigned int numCameras = 0;
-	for (unsigned int id = 0; id < 4 && numCameras < 2; ++id) {
+	/*
+	 * The receivers are walked from the last to the first: on the Surface Go the front camera sits on a later receiver than
+	 * the rear one, and the first camera registered is the one applications offer first (a browser's camera prompt,
+	 * Kamoso). On a tablet that is the camera of video calls and pictures of oneself.
+	 */
+	for (int id = 3; id >= 0 && numCameras < 2; --id) {
 		std::unique_ptr<IPU3CameraData> data =
 			std::make_unique<IPU3CameraData>(this);
 		std::set<Stream *> streams = {
